.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager (lyona)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X. It manages windows in tiled, monocle
and floating layouts. Either layout can be applied dynamically, optimising the
environment for the application in use and the task performed.
.P
In tiled layouts windows are managed in a master and stacking area. The master
area on the left contains one window by default, and the stacking area on the
right contains all other windows. The number of master area windows can be
adjusted from zero to an arbitrary number. In monocle layout all windows are
maximised to the screen size. In floating layout windows can be resized and
moved freely. Dialog windows are always managed floating, regardless of the
layout applied.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
This dwm is lyona's: it draws no bar of its own. Each screen's panel is drawn by
the lyona shell (Quickshell), and dwm keeps windows clear of it. dwm publishes
its tags, layout and windows as root window properties, which the panel reads.
.P
dwm draws a border around windows to indicate the focus state. Closing a window
focuses the window focused before it.
.P
On start, dwm runs the session script autostart.sh in the background, and on
exit it runs autostop.sh and waits up to five seconds for it (see the FILES
section below).  Either may be missing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
The keys and mouse buttons are not compiled in. dwm reads them at run time from
.IR hotkeys.toml
(see FILES), and the modifier is the Super key. Press
.B Super\-/
to see every key binding. Among the defaults:
.TP
.B Super\-r
Open the application launcher.
.TP
.B Super\-q
Close the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-[1..9]
View the windows of a tag;
.B Super\-Shift\-[1..9]
sends the focused window to it.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-q
Log out, after asking.
.B Super\-Control\-Shift\-q
quits dwm at once.
.TP
.B Super\-Button1
Move a window while dragging;
.B Super\-Button3
resizes it.
.P
When neither the user's nor the shipped hotkeys.toml loads at startup, two
emergency keys are bound instead:
.B Super\-x
opens a terminal and
.B Super\-Shift\-q
quits dwm.
.SH FILES
The session scripts are installed in
.IR PREFIX /lib/lyona,
found from the location of the dwm executable
.RI ( PREFIX /bin/dwm).
.TP 15
autostart.sh
Started in the background before dwm enters its handler loop.
.TP 15
autostop.sh
Run when dwm exits; dwm waits up to five seconds for it.
.P
The shipped default hotkeys.toml, themes.toml and window-rules.toml are in
.IR PREFIX /share/lyona/config,
and are used when the files in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyona are missing or unusable.
.TP 15
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyona/hotkeys.toml
Key and mouse bindings.
.TP 15
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyona/themes.toml
The colour theme and border width.
.TP 15
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyona/window-rules.toml
Per-application rules: tag, floating, monitor.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 15
LYONA_DEV_SCRIPTS
A development checkout's scripts directory.  When it holds autostart.sh,
autostop.sh or theme-apply.sh, dwm runs that copy instead of the installed one.
It is never set by an install, and dwm ignores it when running as root.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP 15
SIGUSR1
Reload hotkeys.toml, themes.toml and window-rules.toml.
.TP 15
SIGUSR2
Quit, as the quit binding does.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
Edit the TOML files in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lyona (by default ~/.config/lyona).
dwm watches them and reloads a file when it is saved, without a restart. A file
that does not load is reported, and a running session keeps the configuration
it had. Settings > Appearance changes the theme for you. The
compile-time defaults (layouts, tags, fonts) are in config.h, which needs a
rebuild; the runtime files are the way to customise.
.SH SEE ALSO
The lyona documentation:
.I https://github.com/technicks89/Lyona
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at
.IR https://github.com/technicks89/Lyona/issues .
